            "explanation": "The Argentine Asado (El Asado) and the sacred communal communion of fire and beef:\n- **The Ritual of the Asador (El Asador)**:\n  - The asado is not mere cooking, but an existential social ceremony gathering family and friends every Sunday across Argentina;\n- The Mechanics of Fire & Steel:\n  - **The Wood (La leña)**: Hardwoods like **Quebracho Colorado** and Espinillo burn into long-lasting, aromatic glowing embers (*brasas*);\n  - **Asado a la Cruz / Al Asador**: Splitting an entire side of beef or lamb onto an iron cross angled over an open ground fire, cooking slowly for 4 to 6 hours in the open air;\n  - **La Parrilla (The V-Shaped Iron Grill)**: Angled grates channel rendered beef fat away from the fire into a collection trough, preventing smoke flare-ups;\n- The Canonical Sequence of Cuts:\n  - **1. Achuras (The Offal Prelude)**: Crispy grilled sweetbreads (**mollejas**) drizzled with fresh lemon juice, crunchy chitterlings (**chinchulines**), and grilled morcilla and chorizo sausages (served in crusty bread as **Choripán**);\n  - **2. The Master Cuts**: **Tira de asado** (cross-cut beef short ribs), **Vacío** (flank with crispy exterior fat cap), and **Entraña** (skirt steak with crunchy membrane);\n  - **Accompaniments**: Hand-whisked **Chimichurri** (parsley, oregano, garlic, red pepper flakes, vinegar, oil) and **Salsa Criolla** (diced tomato, onion, and bell pepper);\n  - Concludes with the spontaneous collective tribute: **«¡Un aplauso para el asador!»**.",
