            "explanation": "The regional geometry and culinary art of the Argentine Empanada:\n- **The Argentine Empanada**:\n  - A culinary emblem tracing back to medieval Spain and the Arab world; evolved into distinct, fiercely defended regional styles across the Argentine provinces;\n- Regional Master Styles:\n  - **1. La Empanada Salteña (Salta)**:\n    - Hand-diced beef (*carne cortada a cuchillo*), parboiled diced potatoes (*papa hervida*), hard-boiled eggs, scallions, ground cumin, and spicy ají;\n    - Extremely juicy (*jugosa*), traditionally eaten leaning forward with legs spread (*patas abiertas*) so the dripping savory broth doesn't stain one's shoes;\n  - **2. La Empanada Tucumana (Tucumán - The National Capital of the Empanada)**:\n    - Made strictly with beef flank/rose meat (**matambre**) simmered until tender and diced, lots of green scallions, cumin, and lard;\n    - Zero potatoes and zero olives in purist Tucumán tradition;\n    - Baked in a scorching wood-fired clay oven (**horno de barro**) in under 3 minutes;\n  - **3. La Empanada Mendocina (Mendoza)**: Large ratio of slow-caramelized onions to beef (1:1), seasoned with sweet paprika and black olives;\n- **The Art of the Repulgue (El Repulgue)**:\n  - The manual crimping of the dough edge: master empanada makers fold exactly **13 distinct pleated twists (13 pliegues)** representing Christ and the Twelve Apostles, creating an airtight seal that traps savory juices during baking.",
